e been  on something and you wish you weren't  anymore  so would you like me to say something  yes sir the floor is yours okay so um  you know i've been involved with uh this  concept of recovery for some time  probably all my medical career and  so i've i've thought about recovery and  how it happens  and recovery actually  happens by degrees and if you look  consider the math of  geometry recovery  once you're in a position where you need  to recover  you go in degrees and so the recovery  starts with a 45 degree  change where you stop you withdraw you  avoid  and you hear the common uh  phrase of 28 days it takes in a recovery  mode so that 28 days is the 45  degrees and then you go into a re  rehabilitation phase  which is a 90 degrees and the 90 degrees  is kind of like muscle memory  you you start doing what you learned  to do and then you just go and do that  and you go slow and you've heard the  concept of 90 meetings in 90 days  so it's a 90 degree concept  and then you go into the next phase i  would call it  the return and the return is about 135  degrees  and that's when you you're converted  you say okay i'm doing this well  and i need to help others and that's you  know a six to 12 month  period of time where you you realize you  do care and you are converted and you  are changing  and you've changed and then you you come  to a point where i called  the resume resume your life and you're  at 180 degrees and some people call them  but  talk about 360 degrees i don't want  anybody to go back  a hundred and or 360 degrees because  then you're back where you started  with recovery or with  a problem so you need to resume at 180  days  180 degrees rather  and then you just do life except you do  it better than you've ever done before  when you've recovered  at 180 degrees you're going further  along than you ever were before you've  learned  something you've never learned before  and so you've resumed life  in a better way and it's 180 degrees  going forward instead of going backwards  360.  and then i thought okay so how do you do  it you have  tools that we use from a medical  standpoint for a  mental health standpoint tools such as  medicines like naloxone naltrexone  buprenorphine methadone those are all in  different degrees  and then you have people have friends  you have family of doctors you have  bosses you have the law and then you  have  programs that are available 12  program service oriented uh things and  you have  been a sponsor yourself uh to and then  ultimately in this 180 degree  change you ultimately you have  your higher power you have forgiveness  you learn to forgive yourself  and forgive others and in your process  of forgiving others  you've learned to forgive yourself which  is probably harder than anything else  so as i look at it that's the recovery  process  and that's what we do and how we do it  and then we help others along that road  and that's my simple talk for the day  and open for any questions or  teach me something because i always like  to learn  something okay ryan back to you thank  you  thank you senator hardy thank you so  much for your support of this community  and we  uh look forward to continuing to work  with you and uh thank you for for your  talk on degrees i  i i found that uh very enlightening  myself um  we are going to uh shift to our our next  legislators because we're waiting for  some more to come in  we do have some um pre-recorded messages  uh from legislators who were unable to  join us live this morning and we'll go  ahead and start  uh with senator julia ratty first  um if we could go ahead and there we go  senator ratty hello everyone and welcome  to recovery advocacy day  i so very wish that you could be here  with me in carson city and that we would  be all spending this time together  but i am just so proud of the work  that's been done on recovery issues by  advocates like you  even during the pandemic when we're  using all different kinds of tools to